{
  "lesson_key": "tac-2-the-12-leverage-points",
  "title": "The 12 Leverage Points",
  "course": "TAC",
  "order": 2,
  "lesson_url": "https://agenticengineer.com/tactical-agentic-coding/course/the-12-leverage-points",
  "video": {
    "file": "tac-2-the-12-leverage-points/video/tac-2-the-12-leverage-points.mp4",
    "duration_seconds": 2834,
    "duration_display": "47:14"
  },
  "tactic": {
    "name": "Adopt Your Agent's Perspective",
    "one_liner": "Your agent is brilliant, but blind — ephemeral, context-free, reset to zero after every session — so hand it the information, tools, and resources YOU would use, and ask before every launch: with my agent's core four, can it complete the task I've given it?",
    "why_uncomfortable": "It inverts the engineer's self-image: 'It's not about you anymore. It's about what you can do for your agent so they can work for you.' Instead of being the one who solves the problem, you become the one who sets up the system so a blank-slate agent can solve it — including letting go of watching every step (YOLO mode now, programmable mode from lesson 3 on). Dan names the discomfort directly: engineers love control and visibility, and the hardest thing in TAC is letting that go."
  },
  "mission": "Give you a framework that won't change over time (the compressed phase-two SDLC: plan, code, test, review, document) plus a tactic (adopt your agent's perspective) and a checklist (the 12 leverage points) so you can maximize not what you can do, but what your agents can do — measured by four KPIs: size up, attempts down, streak up, presence down. All of it in service of the TAC mission: becoming the irreplaceable engineer whose codebase increasingly runs itself.",
  "frameworks": {
    "sdlc_phase2": {
      "steps": ["plan", "code", "test", "review", "document"],
      "why": "Tools, models, and compute churn; the software development lifecycle is an aspect of engineering that never changes — 'you can place big bets on it.' TAC uses these five steps as the framework for success and spends the remaining lessons augmenting and automating them so 'your agent ships on your behalf.'"
    },
    "leverage_points": {
      "in_agent": {
        "members": ["context", "model", "prompt", "tools"],
        "definition": "The core four — 'in the agent because they're always with your agent.' On the lesson slide: Context = system prompts and conversation history; Model = LLM selection and configuration; Prompt = user instructions and queries; Tools = available functions and capabilities."
      },
      "through_agent": {
        "members": ["standard out", "types", "documentation", "tests", "architecture", "plans", "templates", "ADWs"],
        "definition": "'External levers that eventually interact with your agent that have massive impact on your success. They typically flow through the context window.' On the lesson slide: Standard Out = response from commands and logging; Types = types, schemas, classes, errors, and structures; Documentation = 3rd party and internal; Tests = self-validating feedback loops; Architecture = codebase structure; Plans = detailed prompts for massive work; Templates = reusable, embeddable, agentic prompts; ADWs = AI Developer Workflows."
      },
      "count_check": "4 in-agent + 8 through-agent = the 12 leverage points of agentic coding."
    },
    "kpis": {
      "size": "Increase. The amount of work handed off per prompt — bigger plans, longer agent runs. Five-minute agentic jobs are good; push toward 10, 20, 30 minutes, an hour, three hours. 'Don't underestimate the amount of work you can hand off autonomously.'",
      "attempts": "Decrease (toward one per prompt). Every post-completion correction costs 'your most important engineering resource: your time.' Attempts of 5–10 (even 3–6) mean a leverage point is missing — find and employ it. Iterating is fine for exploratory work but is not the destination: 'We're not aiming to become a babysitter for AI agents. We're looking for one-shot solutions.'",
      "streak": "Increase. Back-to-back one-shot successes — streaks of 3, 5, 10. Five one-shot prompts that ship features drastically outperform five prompts needing three fixes each ('that's 3x slower'). 'Once you start one-shotting, don't break the streak' — and when it breaks, identify which leverage point was missing.",
      "presence": "Decrease (toward zero) — 'likely the most important.' Presence measures how much you must sit, guide, and correct your agent. Back-and-forth prompting is 'not the end game. This is the early game.' The balancing act: learn what runs at full autonomy (knob at 11) vs where you must still intervene."
    }
  },
  "core_concepts": [
    {
      "id": "sdlc-framework-bet",
      "name": "Bet on what never changes: the phase-two SDLC",
      "plain_english": "Amid tool/model churn, Dan bets the course on the software development lifecycle, compressed to five steps — plan, code, test, review, document — as the framework TAC will augment and automate lesson by lesson until the codebase ships on your behalf.",
      "dan_words": "\"Some aspects of engineering never change... you can place big bets on them... Plan, code, test, review, document. Over the next seven lessons, we're going to augment and automate key steps so that your agent ships on your behalf.\"",
      "misconception": "That the SDLC section is throat-clearing before the real content. Several leverage points ARE SDLC nodes (plans, tests, documentation) — Dan closes by pointing this out; missing the mapping turns a system into a trivia list."
    },
    {
      "id": "adopt-agents-perspective",
      "name": "Tactic #2: Adopt Your Agent's Perspective",
      "plain_english": "Agents boot as blank instances — no context, no memories, no awareness beyond what you provide — and reset to zero when done. To get engineer-grade work, hand the agent your perspective: the information, tools, and resources you would use. Litmus question from the tactic card: with my agent's core four, is it possible to complete the task I've given it?",
      "dan_words": "\"Your agent is brilliant, but blind... Agents are ephemeral, no context, no memories, and no awareness outside of what you give it... Your agent needs your context, your model, your prompt, and your tools, the core four... It's not about what you can do anymore. It's about what you can teach your agents to do.\"",
      "misconception": "Assuming session continuity — that the agent 'remembers' the last session, the open browser, or the error on your screen. Anything not in the context window does not exist; /prime exists precisely because of this."
    },
    {
      "id": "twelve-leverage-points",
      "name": "The 12 leverage points: in-agent vs through-agent",
      "plain_english": "Two families: the core four live IN the agent (context, model, prompt, tools); eight more levers work THROUGH the agent, flowing into the context window (standard out, types, documentation, tests, architecture, plans, templates, ADWs). Stack them and one-shot success becomes inevitable.",
      "dan_words": "\"There are two types of leverage points: in agent and through agent... Through agents are external levers that eventually interact with your agent that have massive impact on your success. They typically flow through the context window... When you stack up these leverage points, Agentic Coding success becomes inevitable.\"",
      "misconception": "Memorizing the list without the diagnostic. The KPI section's drill is the point: when a run fails or a streak breaks, name WHICH leverage point was missing (dicts instead of types? no stdout? no tests? no plan?) and employ it."
    },
    {
      "id": "tools-visibility",
      "name": "Know your agent's tools (or forfeit them)",
      "plain_english": "Claude Code's tools live in its system prompt, so you can just ask (/tools) for the full list with parameters. Reading tool definitions surfaces exploitable details — like Bash's timeout parameter, which later lets Dan run a live server inside the agent for 300 seconds. The toolset CRUDs a codebase just like an engineer.",
      "dan_words": "\"We need to see what our agents can see. We need to know what they can do... did you know that the bash tool here has a timeout?... There are many little hidden kind of secrets inside of every single tool... They can crud — create, read, update, delete — a codebase just like you and I can.\"",
      "misconception": "Never reading tool definitions, then under-using the agent (babysitting terminals outside it) or misreading its behavior (not knowing Task fires a subagent makes the type-trace demo look like magic). MCP servers extend the toolset later — the introspection habit is what transfers."
    },
    {
      "id": "standard-out",
      "name": "Standard out: your agent can only see what you let it see",
      "plain_english": "Stdout is the agent's window into runtime behavior — every command response, error, and log line. The demo app's server swallowed all exceptions into response fields with zero terminal trace: fine-ish for humans watching the browser, invisible to agents. That's bad agentic code.",
      "dan_words": "\"Your agent can only see what you let it see. Standard out is the response from any command you run... clear logging throughout your applications, your commands and your scripts can guide your agent to success... Not only do we have bad code, we have bad agentic code.\"",
      "misconception": "Logging for humans (UI toasts, dashboards, external log services) instead of for agents (stdout/files the agent process can read). Also: fearing stdout overload — Dan gives three escape hatches (per-session log file, cleaned-up logs, warn/error levels) while insisting fuller output shrinks the state space the agent must search."
    },
    {
      "id": "fix-system-not-bug",
      "name": "Set up the codebase so the agent fixes the bug",
      "plain_english": "Dan knows the CSV bug and deliberately does not fix it. Instead he (1) has the agent add [ERROR]/[SUCCESS] prints to every endpoint, (2) runs the server INSIDE a fresh agent via Bash timeout (sh ./scripts/start.sh, 300s), (3) reproduces the error, then asks 'any issues with the server?' — the agent cites file_processor.py:26 and the unsanitized parentheses — and (4) says 'resolve: replace bad chars and escape.' Two short prompts fix it because the system was set up first.",
      "dan_words": "\"I'm not actually fixing the issue. I'm setting up my codebase so that my agent can resolve the issue... We're updating our codebase to communicate information to our agent... Are you still manually looking at error messages? Stop... Give them to your agent.\"",
      "misconception": "Hand-fixing the planted bug (you get a working app and learn nothing), or prompting 'fix the upload bug' BEFORE stdout exists and concluding agents can't debug — when the actual failure was a missing leverage point."
    },
    {
      "id": "types-idks",
      "name": "Types are IDKs (information dense keywords)",
      "plain_english": "Types — classes, exceptions, interfaces, all typing structures — create a traceable flow of information through a codebase. Copy DatabaseSchemaResponse into a context-free agent and ask how it flows: a Task subagent (7 tool uses, 26.7k tokens) returns the exact five-file path from Pydantic model to FastAPI endpoint to mirrored TS interface to API client to the original spec.",
      "dan_words": "\"Types tell a story about how information travels throughout your codebase... Remember that types are IDKs, information dense keywords. They point to exact locations in your code base and represent the flow of a specific type of information.\"",
      "misconception": "Generic names kill the lever: QueryRequest/QueryResponse are 'not that good'; a name like DataRequest is 'terrible' — 'if your agent ever wants to search for data or just request, it's cooked.' Dict-passing codebases have no types to trace at all — the first problem→lever mapping in the KPI drill."
    },
    {
      "id": "architecture-navigation",
      "name": "Architecture and the agent navigation problem",
      "plain_english": "Every fresh agent must re-explore your codebase — hundreds and thousands of boots. Structure is therefore a multiplier: the app/client vs app/server split alone halves the search space. Consistency (same patterns, folders, names, comment structure) is the antidote to complexity; a checklist of concrete practices makes codebases token-efficient and navigable.",
      "dan_words": "\"I've called this the agent navigation problem. Every time you boot up a new agent, it has to explore the codebase... If it's hard for you, you should assume it'll be hard for your agent... consistency is a powerful actor against complexity for both humans and for agents.\"",
      "misconception": "Either ignoring architecture ('the agent will figure it out' — it will, expensively, every boot) or immediately hand-refactoring everything — Dan says 'don't refactor yet': future lessons organize agentic assets WITH your agent."
    },
    {
      "id": "documentation-two-types",
      "name": "Documentation: internal + external, with ai_docs local-first",
      "plain_english": "Internal docs (README, agent-facing markdown spread through the tree) ramp fresh agents; external docs live in ai_docs/ — a folder dedicated to agents holding third-party references (OpenAI/Anthropic quickstarts here) so agents don't burn time and tokens on web requests mid-task.",
      "dan_words": "\"There are two types, internal documentation and external documentation. AI docs is a dedicated folder specifically for our agents. You don't want them making web requests every time they have to look something up. Just get it local.\"",
      "misconception": "Looking for a CLAUDE.md in tac-2 — Dan name-checks the convention, but this repo ships none; README.md + /prime carry the role here. Also assuming the agent 'knows' current APIs from training — the whole point of vendoring quickstarts locally is drift-free, in-context reference."
    },
    {
      "id": "tests-closed-loops",
      "name": "Tests: self-validating feedback loops",
      "plain_english": "Agents will make mistakes; tests are the leverage that lets them catch and correct their own. Run pytest inside the agent (bash mode) and the full output feeds the context window — a closed loop where the agent writes code, validates it, and improves without you relaying errors.",
      "dan_words": "\"Your agent will make mistakes... The only question is, does it have the right leverage it needs to correct the mistakes?... You can create self-validating loops. These are called closed loop structures... If you're not writing tests, you're probably vibe coding.\"",
      "misconception": "Human-in-the-middle testing: running tests yourself and telling the agent what failed throws away the leverage. The loop closes only when the AGENT runs tests and reads results. Backends first (easiest, highest leverage); tests + stdout are the first two levers to install."
    },
    {
      "id": "plans-templates-adws",
      "name": "The abstraction ladder: plans → templates → ADWs",
      "plain_english": "Plans communicate massive batches of work (the 749-line spec bootstrapped this whole app — and Dan didn't type it; lesson 3 teaches planning with your agent). Templates are reusable agentic prompts committed to .claude/commands/ — mint one whenever you repeat a workflow three times (Dan creates /start on camera). ADWs top the ladder: agentic prompts + arbitrary code + an autonomous trigger, seeded by programmable mode (claude -p \"/tools\").",
      "dan_words": "\"Put simply, plans are just prompts. Put accurately, plans are how you communicate massive amounts of work to your agent... Three time makes a pattern... Automate... ADWs are created when you combine one or more agentic prompts right out of the commands directory and then you wrap it in arbitrary code and then you kick it off autonomously with a trigger.\"",
      "misconception": "Ladder inversions: hand-typing plans; hoarding one-off prompts outside the repo; or chasing ADWs before single-prompt mastery — 'we must know how to fire off a single powerful agent running a single prompt... Master the primitives and you'll master the compositions.'"
    },
    {
      "id": "kpis-and-letting-go",
      "name": "Four KPIs + the phase-one confession",
      "plain_english": "Track exactly four numbers — size (up), attempts (down toward 1), streak (up), presence (down toward 0) — and diagnose every failure back to a missing leverage point. Then the reveal: the whole lesson was Claude Code in 'phase one mode' (iterative back-and-forth), which tanks presence; from lesson 3 on, TAC shifts to programmable mode and the uncomfortable discipline of letting go of control.",
      "dan_words": "\"If you don't measure it, you can't improve it... We have size, attempts, we have streak, and we have presence. This is it. This is all we need to track to win... We've been using Claude Code in phase one mode... This tanks our presence KPI... In order to let our code base run itself, we have to let go of control.\"",
      "misconception": "Reading 'iteration is bad' as absolute (exploratory iteration is fine; attempts rise temporarily when size scales — the sin is accepting iteration as the permanent workflow), or mistaking presence for productivity (feeling productive BECAUSE you watch every token is the early-game trap)."
    }
  ],
