    "prompt": "User instructions and queries. This lesson's prompts are deliberately small (two-line fixes, slash commands) because the heavy lifting moves into through-agent levers; lesson 3 scales prompts into plans.",
    "tools": "Available functions and capabilities — introspectable via /tools since they're embedded in the system prompt. The Bash timeout parameter is the lesson's proof that tool-definition literacy pays: it enables running the app server inside the agent.",
    "why_scale_matters": "The core four are re-framed as the IN-AGENT half of the leverage-point taxonomy: 'your agent needs YOUR context, YOUR model, YOUR prompt, and YOUR tools.' Adopting the agent's perspective means auditing all four before every launch — the tactic card's litmus question — because they're always present whether you attend to them or not."
  },
  "stdout_demo": {
    "before_summary": "Committed repo state: app/server/server.py has six endpoints and zero print() calls; every handler catches exceptions and returns them as response-model error fields. The browser shows 'Error converting CSV to SQLite: near \"(\": syntax error' when orders_(32).csv is uploaded, but the server terminal shows nothing. Root cause is planted: server.py:60 derives the table name replacing only spaces, so parentheses survive; file_processor.py:26 then runs PRAGMA table_info(orders_(32)) — invalid SQLite.",
    "after_summary": "Agent-performed fixes (NOT committed — the student reproduces them): (1) [ERROR] prints with full tracebacks + [SUCCESS] prints before every return across all endpoints ('Updated server.py to print detailed exception information and successful response details for all endpoints'); (2) sanitize_table_name() in core/file_processor.py (21 additions: strip extension, re.sub non-alphanumerics to underscores, ensure leading letter/underscore, never empty). After restart: upload succeeds, 'latest delivery' query works.",
    "difference_that_matters": "Same bug, same agent — the only change between 'agent can't help here at all' and a two-prompt fix ('any issues with the server?' → 'resolve: replace bad chars and escape') is stdout visibility plus running the server inside the agent (Bash timeout 300s). The leverage point, not the model, was the bottleneck. This is Tactic #2 made operational: the litmus question failed, the system was upgraded, then it passed.",
    "codebase_paths": [
      "app/server/server.py",
      "app/server/core/file_processor.py",
      "app/client/public/sample-data/orders_(32).csv",
      "scripts/start.sh"
    ]
  },
