  "teach_back_prompt": "Explain to a colleague, without notes: (1) the two families of leverage points and all 12 members, and why 'through-agent' levers all route through the context window; (2) the stdout demo end-to-end — what made the server bad agentic code, why Dan refused to fix the bug directly, what 'sh ./scripts/start.sh, 300s' exploited, and the two short prompts that fixed it; (3) the four KPIs with directions, and how a broken streak turns into a leverage-point diagnosis; and (4) the tactic that summarizes it all — adopt your agent's perspective — including its litmus question. Finish with the lesson's closing paradox: why does dropping YOUR presence (letting go of control) increase what gets engineered?",
  "retrieval_questions": [
    { "q": "Name the two families of leverage points and all 12 members.", "a": "In-agent (core four): context, model, prompt, tools. Through-agent: standard out, types, documentation, tests, architecture, plans, templates, ADWs." },
    { "q": "State Tactic #2 and its litmus question.", "a": "Adopt your agent's perspective. Before every launch: 'With my agent's core four (context, model, prompt, tools), is it possible to complete the task I've given it?'" },
    { "q": "What are the five steps of the phase-two SDLC, and why bet on the SDLC at all?", "a": "Plan, code, test, review, document — the SDLC is an aspect of engineering that never changes, so it survives tool/model churn." },
    { "q": "Why was the demo server 'bad agentic code,' and what exactly was the planted bug?", "a": "Every endpoint swallowed exceptions into response error fields with zero stdout, so agents couldn't see failures. The upload path only replaces spaces when deriving table names, so orders_(32).csv → table orders_(32) → PRAGMA table_info fails at file_processor.py:26 with an SQLite syntax error near '('." },
    { "q": "What command ran the app inside the agent, and which tool detail made it possible?", "a": "sh ./scripts/start.sh, 300s — exploiting the Bash tool's timeout parameter Dan surfaced by reading /tools output." },
    { "q": "Reproduce Dan's two bug-fixing prompts and say why they sufficed.", "a": "'Any issues with the server?' then 'resolve: replace bad chars and escape.' They sufficed because the system had been set up first: stdout logging everywhere + the server running inside the agent's context." },
    { "q": "What does IDK stand for, and through which five files does DatabaseSchemaResponse flow?", "a": "Information dense keywords. core/data_models.py → server.py → client/src/types.d.ts → client/src/api/client.ts → specs/init_nlq_to_sql_to_table.md." },
    { "q": "What is the agent navigation problem and its antidote?", "a": "Every fresh agent boot must re-explore the codebase (hundreds/thousands of times), so structure compounds for or against you; the antidote is consistency — same patterns, folder structures, function/file names, comment structure." },
    { "q": "List five items from Dan's codebase-architecture checklist.", "a": "Any five of: clear entry points; constant files for unchanging info; types/classes/structures; meaningful verbose IDK names; files under ~1000 lines; agent-facing markdown (README/CLAUDE.md) throughout the tree; separated services; one responsibility per file; tests mirroring source structure." },
    { "q": "What are the two types of documentation, and what is ai_docs/ for?", "a": "Internal (codebase docs like README/CLAUDE.md) and external (third-party). ai_docs/ is a dedicated agent-facing folder of local external docs so agents don't make web requests every lookup." },
